WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
3.5 million scrubber bonuses kept high sulfur coal units running longer. This is Unintended Consequences, episode 156. Extension credits that outlived their bridge, the Midwest bargain behind them, a lottery and a soft price. Plants that stayed, later caps that carried the cut, and the design question those credits still pose. On January 1, 1995, the first phase 1 units under the 1990 Clean Air Act amendments began receiving allowances under Title 4.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:36.680
The statute had offered a two-year compliance extension, out to 1997, to any unit that

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:43.500
installed high-efficiency scrubbers. Phase I covered 261 generating units at 110 plants.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.920
The Energy Information Administration later recorded that American electric power and

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.740
the Tennessee Valley Authority together received more than 1.4 million of those extension

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:59.840
allowances. Those two utilities banked the credits or moved them, and high sulfur units kept

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.700
running. A bonus written as a bridge became operating cash for the fleet the cap was

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.400
meant to turn over. The political logic that produced those allowances begins in the

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:15.140
Midwest coal fields in the bargaining over the 1990 amendments. Lawmakers had a stubborn

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:20.120
problem on the table. Acid rain was damaging lakes and forests across the northeast.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.980
Many Midwestern utilities and coal state representatives opposed any rule that would force rapid

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:32.960
shutdowns of high sulfur mines. That opposition was not a mystery. Local coal, local jobs, and local

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.040
plants where the stake those representatives were sent to defend. The compromise embedded in

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:43.760
Title IV tried to hold both facts at once. It created a national cap on sulfur dioxide

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.200
and allowed trading of allowances. It also added special bonus allowances and compliance

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.760
extensions for plants that installed flue gas de-sulfurization equipment. The hardware known as

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:00.500
The Environmental Protection Agency's own 1990 summary described the goal as a permanent

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.400
10 million ton reduction from 1980 levels, achieved through flexible market-based incentives

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.340
rather than plant by plant mandates.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.000
At the time, analysts expected a fairly clean choice.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.120
Utilities would switch to low sulfur coal, or they would install scrubbers and then trade

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.480
any excess allowances.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.960
Either path, the modeling suggested, would accelerate retirement of the oldest, dirtiest units.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.000
The approach looked rational with the information then in hand. Allowance prices

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.740
were projected to be high enough to favor fuel switching or early retirement in many cases.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.940
The bonus allowances were framed as a narrow time-limited bridge for politically sensitive

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:45.380
regions. Illinois, Indiana and Ohio each received an additional allocation of 200,000 annual

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:51.200
allowances each year during Phase 1. That provision was written explicitly to ease the transition

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.560
for plants burning local high sulfur coal, while the overall tonnage cap still had to be met.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:01.080
Nobody in that room was trying to preserve the dirtiest units as a permanent class.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:05.300
They were trying to get a national cut through a region that could block the bill.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.180
A market plus a bridge looked kinder than a shutdown order, and still pointed at the tons.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:15.940
With the rules written, the Environmental Protection Agency ran the lottery that would

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:21.020
distribute the extension credits. The lottery did not pick winners at random in the carnival

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:26.680
sense. It distributed the allowances on the basis of which units committed to 90% or greater

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.380
sulfur dioxide removal. Commit to that removal rate, install the scrubber, and the unit entered

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:38.300
the pool for extension allowances. The result was an immediate stock of credits. A utility could

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:44.220
use that stock inside its own system or sell it to someone else. Early monitoring data showed rapid

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.700
overcompliance and a lot of allowance banking. Supporters cited that pattern as proof the market

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:50.880
mechanism was working.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.300
Plants were cutting more sulfur dioxide than the near-term cap required, and the unused

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.560
rights were being saved.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.820
In a trading program, a bank can be a sign of health.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.120
It can mean reductions arrived early.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.640
It can also mean the future cap has already been partly spent.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.880
A smaller number of plants than originally forecast chose to install scrubbers once allowance

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.680
prices fell below expectations.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.200
The plants that did install them captured large volumes of tradable credits.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.100
So, the technology the bonus was written to spread did not spread as widely as the early

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.220
forecast assumed.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.360
Where it did spread, it minted a thick stack of rights.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:28.980
Price was the hinge.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.400
Designers had expected prices high enough to push fuel switching or retirement.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.840
When prices came in lower, the arithmetic inside the utility changed, without anyone

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:39.980
rewriting the statute.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.980
Because the extension allowances were tied to the act of installing scrubbers, not to

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.080
the permanent retirement of high emitting capacity. A second use appeared. Utilities

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.760
found they could keep older, less efficient, high-sulfur units online, and still meet

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.760
their caps by using the bonus credits at other locations. The scrubber did its local job,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:05.060
the credit did a different job, somewhere else on the wires. An analysis in the Tulane

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:10.420
Environmental Law Journal noted one large operator in particular. That operator generated

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:15.920
more than 1 million excess allowances at a single scrubbed plant, then bank them or transfer

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.560
them to units that continued burning local high sulfur coal. One clean stack in that telling

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.000
underwrote a scatter of stacks that did not clean up in the same way. This spatial shift moved

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.200
emissions away from the scrubbed site. It preserved or even increased total emissions in states

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:38.140
without stringent local air quality rules. The cap was national. The harm people breathe and the

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:40.720
harm that falls on lakes are not evenly national.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.040
A ton removed at a scrubbed plant

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.780
and a ton still released downwind

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.540
are not the same ton to the forest at the end of the wind.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:50.360
The brief record does not give a single lake

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:51.920
by lake ledger of that shift.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:55.560
It does say the consequences for downwind deposition patterns

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.640
in the eastern United States were measurable.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:00.780
The same bonus structure changed the replacement math.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.320
It reduced the relative cost of continuing

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.360
to operate older plants, instead of replacing them

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.960
with newer, more efficient units.

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.100
Those newer units would have emitted less even without scrubbers, so the incentive was

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.420
not only clean this stack, it was also this stack once scrubbed and credited, is cheaper

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.780
to keep than a replacement that never needed the bonus.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:25.060
Over time the banking of millions of allowances created a stockpile.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:29.840
That stockpile delayed the stringency of phase two limits beginning in 2000.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.660
Phase two was supposed to be the tighter chapter.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:38.080
A fat bank meant the tighter chapter could be met for a while by spending yesterday's

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:40.200
surplus rather than by turning the fleet over.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.260
The net result was slower turnover of the coal fleet than the original modeling had assumed.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.720
When allowance prices stayed low, the economic case for early retirement weakened further.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.060
A plant that had already received its extension allowances could operate profitably for additional

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:01.180
years by purchasing cheap credits rather than investing in a new combined cycle unit.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.480
Retirement is a capital decision.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.780
Cheap credits are an operating expense.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.760
If the operating expense stays small, the capital decision waits.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:13.240
The second order effect showed up in state-level permitting.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.340
Regulators in coal-dependent regions approved life extension projects for units that would

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.680
have faced stricter scrutiny without the cushion of tradable allowances.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:24.460
A life extension is not a dramatic headline.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:29.720
It is a permit, a repair budget, a few more years on a unit everyone already knows how

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.060
to run.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:35.240
one. Stack those permits across a region, and the fleet the 1990 model expected to shrink

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:40.780
stays familiar. None of this required a utility to break the cap. The cap was met in the

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:45.120
accounting sense by credits the statute itself had printed for doing the favored thing.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.940
That is the uncomfortable part, and it is worth sitting with. The people who wrote the

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:54.300
bridge wanted the tons down, and they wanted Midwestern votes for the bill that would

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.940
get the tons down. The people who installed scrubbers did the thing the statute paid them

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:04.080
to do. The surprise was in the link. Pay for the equipment and you may pay the equipment's

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.200
host to remain. By the late 1990s, the scale of that distortion was visible in the price

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.500
and in the fleet. Allowance prices remained lower than projected. Scrubber-driven extensions

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:19.800
had preserved more high sulfur capacity than anticipated. The response was not a repeal.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:24.660
Subsequent tightening came through separate rules, the clean air interstate rule, and later

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:29.880
the cross-state air pollution rule. Those rules imposed additional caps. They effectively

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:35.020
retired many of the units that had benefited from the original bonus allowances. The acid

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:40.260
rain program itself was never formally repealed. Its cap was allowed to tighten through successive

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:45.560
regulatory layers. The original trading system stayed on the books. The work of finishing

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:50.880
the cut moved in practice into the layers above it. Today, the power sector sulfur dioxide

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:57.920
Inventory stands roughly 94% below 1990 levels, according to environmental protection agency data.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:03.500
Picture the 1990 plume from the power sector, then picture nearly all of that plume gone.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.560
That is a real victory for lakes, forests and lungs, and it should be said that way.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.720
The path to that reduction ran through additional command and control measures,

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.300
layered on top of the original trading system.

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:19.340
The original extension allowances had already been used or banked,

00:09:19.340 --> 00:09:24.140
So, the later rules had to address both the remaining emissions and the accumulated bank.

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:27.200
A credit used is a ton already spoken for.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.020
A credit bank is a ton still available to be spoken for later.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:36.280
Either way, a later cap that ignores the bank is a cap that has not yet met the past.

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:40.860
There is a meta irony in the repair, and it is not a joke at anyone's expense.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:46.180
A program praised for flexibility needed tighter, more specific rules to retire the

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.700
capacity its own bonus had helped keep online.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.720
The market did what its prices told it to do.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:56.580
The prices, shaped by the bonus and by over compliance, told it to wait.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:01.600
When regulators allocate extra allowances specifically to plants that adopt a particular

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.040
control technology, they create a direct financial incentive to keep the controlled units operating

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:08.460
rather than to retire them.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:11.580
In this program that incentive was not abstract.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:16.500
fact, it was an extension credit usable at the scrubbed plant or transferable to a high

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.260
sulfur unit that had not retired.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:22.780
What was measured was installation and removal rate at the unit that volunteered.

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:27.880
What was not measured for the purpose of the bonus was whether that unit or its sister

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:29.100
units left the fleet.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.640
Later programs that set technology-neutral declining caps, or that paired trading with

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.800
explicit retirement schedules, avoided repeating the same extension effect.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.900
A declining cap that does not care which machine you use does not pay you extra for owning

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:44.720
the old machine.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.520
A retirement schedule says the credit ends when the source ends.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:54.480
If you follow how a cap on paper becomes a plume in a downwind state, environmental intelligence

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.640
is the narrow show that stays with those regulatory ledgers.

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:00.980
The question is not whether scrubbers were a bad machine.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.540
They remove sulfur dioxide at the stacks that installed them.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.260
The question is what the bonus was attached to.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:11.900
you. Watch a new emissions trading design the way you would watch a repair contract on

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:16.620
an old boiler. If the payment arrives for fitting the part and keeps arriving while

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:21.820
the boiler stays in the building, ask who is being paid to remain. Would you rather the

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:26.600
bonus die with the unit or travel with the unit into the next decade? That's the case

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:31.820
for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:36.580
who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening. See you tomorrow, and

